{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nMutation testing should protect changed behavioral cores, but the historical whole-file counts are stale. A pinned mutmut 3.7.0 probe found 814 mutants in `signing.py` versus 491 in the old record, including both equivalent survivors and real contract gaps. Enforcing the old ceilings would block unrelated work; accepting all current survivors would normalize defects. The approved rollout therefore starts with evidence gathering and promotes each shard only when its results and runtime justify stronger enforcement.\n\n## Plan\n\nUse `.github/mutation-shards.toml` as the sole source for shard mappings, reviewed equivalent fingerprints, explicit timeout inputs, and an explicit per-shard enforcement mode:\n\n- `advisory`: run head mutation evidence, publish every outcome, and do not fail Gerrit for mutation-quality findings. Configuration, selection, execution, and artifact-contract failures still fail because usable evidence was not produced.\n- `ratchet`: compare fresh base/head archives. For AST-identical functions, preserve unchanged legacy survivors and `no tests` outcomes. Block killed mutants that disappear or become survived, timed out, incomplete, or `no tests`; block new survivors, timeouts, and incomplete statuses. Report newly introduced `no tests` without blocking because mutmut cannot reliably attribute subprocess tests. Added or edited functions must kill every mutant unless a survivor matches an individually reviewed equivalent fingerprint; `no tests` remains report-only in ratchet.\n- `strict`: apply the changed-function rule to the entire shard. Every mutant must be killed or individually fingerprinted as equivalent; `no tests`, timeouts, incomplete statuses, and zero mutants block. Strict promotion therefore requires direct in-process mutation coverage.\n\nStart all legacy shards as advisory. Promote a shard to ratchet only after three successful base/head pilot runs show stable mappings and a maximum runtime of 24 minutes or less inside its 30-minute job. If a shard cannot meet that threshold, narrow it to proven function patterns before promotion. Promote it to strict only after legacy non-killed outcomes are remediated or individually reviewed. Mode changes are small manifest reviews; there is no fuzzy severity classification.\n\nA selector inspects `HEAD^..HEAD`, including both sides of renames. Mapped source, test, or support changes select their shards; mutation-infrastructure changes select all. Unmapped changed Python tests are reported as advisory findings. Unresolved refs or diffs fail; an empty selection is an explicit successful skip. The reusable workflow emits selector JSON and creates one matrix job per selected shard, so every shard has its own 30-minute cap. Advisory evaluates head only; ratchet evaluates fresh base and head archives; strict evaluates complete head evidence plus the regression evidence required by the driver.\n\nFor each archive the driver synthesizes `[tool.mutmut]` from the canonical manifest before execution. The repository does not retain a second global `[tool.mutmut]` shard mapping. The manifest pins `timeout_constant = 1.0` and `timeout_multiplier = 15.0`; mutmut therefore gives each mutant a wall limit of `(estimated selected-test runtime + 1 second) * 15`, while the workflow independently caps the shard job at 30 minutes. Each job uses Python 3.12, pinned `mutmut==3.7.0`, `--max-children 1`, and selected clean-test preflight. Dependency downloads may be cached; mutation verdict state may not. Gerrit checks out the exact patchset and includes mutation in `vote.needs`; the push/PR lane mirrors it. A weekly/manual sweep selects every shard through the same selector and matrix.\n\nMutation-result reruns are diagnostic only and cannot turn red green. Only independently identified setup failures may retry. Raw logs, survivor diffs and fingerprints, and a structured summary publish for every driver outcome. Documentation describes the live manifest policy and promotion process and removes stale score or budget tables. A contract test reads the documentation, asserts the required policy sections and mode names, and asserts the historical score-table heading is absent. The existing two-vote rollback runbook remains the emergency recovery path.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Selection maps changed source, test, support, and global inputs to the expected shard set.\n- [ ] Selection keeps both sides of renames and deletions, reports unmatched Python tests, returns an explicit empty selection, fails unresolved refs, and emits selector JSON for targeted or all-shard runs.\n- [ ] Manifest loading accepts only `advisory`, `ratchet`, or `strict`, emits the pinned timeout values into each archive config, and no duplicate repository mutmut mapping remains.\n- [ ] Advisory returns success for survived, `no tests`, timeout, and incomplete mutation findings while returning failure for missing evidence or configuration and execution errors.\n- [ ] Ratchet preserves unchanged legacy survivors and `no tests` and reports newly introduced `no tests` without blocking.\n- [ ] Ratchet blocks killed regressions, missing killed mutants, new survivors, timeouts, and incomplete statuses; added or edited functions require killed mutants or individually reviewed equivalent fingerprints except that `no tests` is report-only.\n- [ ] Strict requires every mutant to be killed or individually fingerprinted and blocks every `no tests`, timeout, incomplete status, and zero-mutant run.\n- [ ] Execution uses fresh archives, restores no verdict cache, pins runtime and concurrency, runs clean-test preflight, generates archive-local config, and always writes structured evidence.\n- [ ] Workflows use selector-derived per-shard matrix jobs with a 30-minute limit each, exact Gerrit patchset checkout, `vote.needs`, push/PR parity, weekly/manual all-shard selection, cancellation, and always-published artifacts.\n- [ ] A real compact-policy smoke run passes from fresh base/head archives.\n- [ ] The documentation contract test proves the phased modes, deterministic promotion evidence, false-positive policy, cadence, manifest authority, and absence of stale score or budget tables.\n\n## Verification\n\n    pytest -q tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py\n    python scripts/mutation_gate.py smoke --shard compact-policy --base HEAD^ --head HEAD\n    python scripts/check_verify_gate_parity.py\n    make config-check\n    make lint\n    make typecheck\n\n## Primary evidence\n\nCurrent primary-source examples: Instructor uses a pinned bounded shard with explicit exceptions; DataDog maps changed tests to source and compares base/head; PostHog gates a targeted PR shard; Trailmark cancels superseded runs and always reports; GeoParquet schedules broad mutation, fails on missing evidence, retries setup only, and publishes artifacts. The pinned mutmut 3.7.0 probe verifies `run --help` exposes `--max-children`; `configuration.py` defaults and fingerprints `timeout_constant=1.0` and `timeout_multiplier=15.0`; `__main__.py` derives the wall limit from those values, maps exit codes to every consumed status, and produces survivor output with `difflib.unified_diff`. The real repository probe and corrections are recorded in concise ticket comments."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786425104920724001,"uuid":"4565b921-f690-4a58-8f36-0dca516cda94"}